Cannabis-infused Coconut oil is another staple infusion in the cannabis kitchen. Infused oil works in nearly any recipe that calls for coconut oil. Coconut oil and [olive oil](/edibles/cannabis-infused-olive-oil) make a great vegan alternative to [butter](/edibles/cannabis-infused-butter-cannabutter)!

If you're new to edibles, check out our [cannabis edibles for beginners guide](/edibles/cooking-with-cannabis-edibles-101) to learn the basics.

If you're looking for butter, check out our [cannabis-infused butter how-to](/edibles/cannabis-infused-butter-cannabutter).

If you're looking for olive oil, it's the same process, but here's the guide for [cannabis-infused olive oil](/edibles/cannabis-infused-olive-oil).

## How Much Decarbed Weed Should I Use?

You always want to use decarboxylated cannabis for your infusions. See our [cannabis decarb how-to](/edibles/decarb-activate-cannabis-edibles) to learn how to decarb your material.

- **Flower:** Try 14 to 28 grams of decarbed weed per cup of oil.
- **Concentrates:** Try 3 to 6 grams decarbed concentrate per cup of oil. To infuse, gently heat your oil and mix your decarbed concentrate directly into the oil. **Never microwave your cannabis products!**
- **Kief:** Try 7 to 14 grams of decarbed cannabis per cup of oil.
- **Trim and Shake:** Try 28 to 48 grams, decarbed per cup of oil.

For help with dosing, check out our [cannabis edibles dosing calculator](/edibles-calculator).

### Note on Oil Loss

When infusing oil with flowers, some oil will stay in the bud, meaning some loss of your original quantity of oil. For this reason, I will often compensate by using 1.25 cups for each cup of oil I want in the end.

## How to Make Cannabis-Infused Coconut Oil in a Crockpot

1. Lay a clean {% affiliateLink slug="tea-towel" /%} in the bottom of your {% affiliateLink slug="crockpot" /%} to buffer the glass jar against the bottom of the crock. Add enough water to the crockpot to come at least 3/4 of the way to the top of your jar(s).
2. Stick a digital thermometer in your water bath and turn the crockpot on high. Allow the water bath to come up to 185°F and then turn to low.
3. While the crockpot is heating up, decarb your cannabis if you haven't already. Add the oil and decarboxylated cannabis to a clean mason jar and screw on the lid finger tight.
4. Place your sealed jar in the crock and cook on low for four hours, maintaining a temperature of 185°F.
5. After four hours strain your oil and weed through a fine-mesh strainer/cheesecloth into a clean container or jar. See our [straining notes](#straining-cannabis-infused-coconut-oil) below.
6. Allow the oil to cool.
7. Enjoy your oil, use it in [recipes](/edibles/), or see notes below for [storage information](#storing-cannabis-infused-oil).

## How to Make Cannabis-Infused Coconut Oil on a Stovetop

1. Combine your cannabis, oil, and two cups of water in a pot and bring to a gentle simmer. The water helps buffer temperature fluctuations. Take care not to exceed 210°F to preserve precious cannabinoids. A {% affiliateLink slug="candy-thermometer" /%} helps monitor this. Around 200°F is the goal.
2. Gently simmer your material for four hours.
3. Strain your oil into a container and allow it to cool. See our [straining notes](#straining-cannabis-infused-coconut-oil) below.
4. Enjoy your oil, use it in [recipes](/edibles/), or see [storage notes](#storing-cannabis-infused-oil) below.

## How to Make Cannabis-Infused Coconut Oil in an Infusion Device

You can simplify the infusion process by using an infusion machine such as the {% affiliateLink slug="levo2" text="LEVO" /%}, Ardent FX, or Magical Butter. These machines make infusion as easy as loading your cannabis and butter/oil into the chamber and pressing a button. The {% affiliateLink slug="levo2" text="LEVO" /%} and Ardent can even decarb your cannabis!

## Straining Cannabis-Infused Coconut Oil

Straining the plant material from your oil helps create a more pure, longer-lasting, better-tasting product. You'll want to use something food-safe like {% affiliateLink slug="coffee-filters" /%} and a {% affiliateLink slug="funnel" /%}, a {% affiliateLink slug="fine-mesh-strainer" /%}, {% affiliateLink slug="cheesecloth" /%}, or my personal favorite: a {% affiliateLink slug="bubble-bag" /%}. I like the bubble bags best because they don't absorb nearly much of my prized oil as cheesecloth or a coffee filter is prone to absorbing.

## Storing Cannabis-Infused Oil

Store your coconut oil in an air-tight container in the fridge for a month or freeze for several months. Cannabinoids do degrade over time, so it is best to use your oil soon.
